{
  "days": {
    "vr.p.kausalya_consents": {
      "for_you": "You are caught between two homes, and the guilt of staying weighs on you like a heavy stone. Kausalya's story shows you that love is not always about being physically present at the bedside. Sometimes, the deepest care is the quiet, steady choice to protect the one you are with. Your mother is not in the room with you, but your presence with your family is its own form of devotion. You do not have to carry the verdict of your choice as a sentence. You are allowed to be where you are, doing what you can."
    }
  }
}

{
  "days": {
    "bg.2.27": {
      "meaning": "Krishna speaks to Arjuna on the battlefield, where grief has stopped him from acting. He reminds him that what is truly alive in us never comes into being and never passes away. The body and its losses are temporary. This does not mean we should stop caring for those who are sick. It means the core of who you are remains, even when everything around you changes.",
      "for_you": "Your mother’s body went through a surgery, and that pain was real. But the part of you that loves her and the part of her that is not just a body are not defined by that moment. You can let the verdict against yourself loosen its grip."
    },
    "mbh.3.210.59": {
      "meaning": "In Mithila, a meat-seller named Dharmavyadha tells a proud ascetic that true wisdom is found in doing honest work and caring for your parents. He says that if someone has done wrong and then turns to doing good, they are freed from that wrong, like the moon emerging from a cloud.",
      "for_you": "You are doing the work you have. You are raising your children and building your life. That is your honest work. Let that be enough, and let it be the good you are doing now."
    },
    "mbh.5.133.7": {
      "meaning": "Queen Vidula finds her son Sanjaya lying defeated after losing his kingdom. She tells him not to hold himself in contempt or settle for little. She urges him to set his mind on what is truly good and to stop fearing, to pull himself together and act.",
      "for_you": "You are not defeated, only stretched. You have not settled for little; you are carrying a great deal. Set your mind on the good you are doing right now, and let that quiet the voice that says you did not do enough."
    }
  }
}

{
  "days": {
    "bg.18.47": {
      "meaning": "Krishna is speaking to Arjuna on the field of battle. The armies are drawn up and the moment of choice is heavy. He tells Arjuna that it is better to walk the path that is his own, even if it is flawed and uncertain, than to take up another's duty and perform it perfectly. The work that comes from your own nature, he says, carries its own rightness. It asks you to offer what you are, with all its limits, as the work itself.",
      "for_you": "You are trying to perform a role that was not yours to carry. The work of your own life is here, in the middle of your days. You are building your own thing, raising your children, and caring for your parents. That is your path. It is imperfect, as all paths are, but it is yours. Let it be enough."
    },
    "yv.1.26.11": {
      "meaning": "Rama is speaking to Vishvamitra and Vasishtha in a moment of deep weariness. He has seen the world and feels the weight of it. He says that our own senses are the enemies. Truth itself feels untrue. The self strikes the self, and the mind becomes the mind's own enemy. He is naming the pain of a mind that turns against itself, a mind that cannot find rest.",
      "for_you": "The mind that replays your choice is your own mind, turning against itself. It is not an enemy outside of you. It is the part of you that is trying to understand, but is only finding the same old loop. You are not broken. You are a mind in a loop, and it is tired."
    },
    "mbh.12.338.12": {
      "meaning": "Narada is speaking to Shuka, who is in deep sorrow. The pain is there, and nothing can be done to change the past. Narada tells him that when pain arrives and nothing can be done, do not keep thinking about it. Not dwelling on it is the medicine for sorrow. Brooding does not make the pain leave; it only makes it grow. The mind, he says, must be cared for as the body is.",
      "for_you": "The operation is over. What is done is done. The mind now turns to the night, to the replay, to the verdict. But the medicine is not in the replay. It is in the letting go. The mind is a thing that needs rest, just as the body does. Give it that rest."
    }
  }
}

{
  "days": {
    "mbh.p.pingala": {
      "for_you": "You wait for your mind to quiet down so you can rest. Pingala shows you that waiting for someone else to bring you peace is a habit that keeps you awake. You might find rest not by forcing the thoughts away, but by letting go of the need for them to stop."
    }
  }
}

{
  "days": {
    "bg.6.26": {
      "meaning": "Krishna is showing Arjuna how to sit with his mind. He tells him that the mind will wander. When it does, Arjuna should gently guide it back. This is not a one-time fix. It is a practice of moderation and gentle return, again and again. The mind is like a lamp that needs steadying, and the work is simply to bring it home.",
      "for_you": "When you lie in bed and your mind is drafting tomorrow's emails, you do not need to fight it. You only need to notice where it has gone, and gently bring it back. This is your part. Do it again, and again, without force."
    },
    "up_maha.109": {
      "meaning": "Nidagha is sitting with his teacher Ribhu. He is frustrated. He tells Ribhu that his mind runs here and there for no reason. It wanders far away, like a stray dog in a village. Ribhu listens. He knows that naming this restlessness is the first step toward understanding it. The wandering is not a failure; it is simply what the mind does.",
      "for_you": "You see the pattern now. Your mind runs here and there, chasing tomorrow, not because you are failing, but because it is its nature. Naming this is the beginning. It is not a verdict on you; it is simply the truth of what is happening."
    },
    "ashtavakra.18.58": {
      "meaning": "Ashtavakra is speaking to King Janaka about the calm of the wise. He says the fool is restless even when doing nothing. But the skilled one stays calm even when doing everything. This calm is not found by running away or chasing peace. It is found in how you hold your ordinary life.",
      "for_you": "You are skilled. You do not need to find a new place or a new routine to be calm. You need to hold your ordinary life, with all its weight, with a steadiness that does not depend on the outcome."
    }
  }
}

{
  "days": {
    "up_kath.1.3.5": {
      "meaning": "Yama is still speaking to Nachiketa, using the chariot to show what happens when a person is not awake inside themselves. The self is the rider. The body is the chariot. The intellect is the driver. The mind is the reins. The senses are the horses. If the mind is unsteady, the reins drop and the horses bolt. If the mind is steady, the horses obey and go where they are meant to go. The choice is always yours to hold the reins.",
      "for_you": "At night, your mind is the driver without reins. The phone is the horse running wild. Before you sleep, put the phone out of reach and sit still. Let your mind know it is safe to stop. Let it see the night is not a task to finish."
    },
    "bh.vairagya.62": {
      "meaning": "Bhartrihari speaks to his own mind from a place of deep fatigue. He asks why it wanders in vain, replaying what is gone and scripting what is not yet here. He tells it to rest right where it is. He says whatever is to happen will happen, and he will take what comes without looking for it. The calm he wants is not somewhere else; it is here, in this moment, without the noise.",
      "for_you": "Tonight, when you feel the urge to draft tomorrow's emails, tell your mind what Bhartrihari told his: rest. What is to happen will happen. You have done your part for the day. Let the phone stay dark and let your mind simply be."
    },
    "viv.1.wait_and_watch": {
      "meaning": "Swami Vivekananda is teaching his students in New York about the first step with a racing mind. He says the mind is like a monkey, jumping and restless, full of thoughts and desires. You do not fight it. You simply sit and let it jump. You wait and watch. Each day you do this, the monkey jumps less. The mind grows calmer on its own, without force.",
      "for_you": "Tomorrow morning, sit for five minutes before you look at your phone. Let the monkey jump. Do not try to stop the thoughts. Just watch them. See what happens when you do not grab the reins but simply wait and watch."
    }
  }
}

{
  "days": {
    "mbh.p.chirakari": {
      "for_you": "Your mind is replaying the fight with your daughter like a film you cannot pause. This story shows that when anger flares and words are thrown, the wisest move is to slow down. Let the next hour pass before you speak to her again."
    }
  }
}

{
  "days": {
    "bg.11.44": {
      "meaning": "Arjuna is on his knees. He has just seen the endless forms of the universe, and he is terrified. He bows to Krishna and asks him to forgive his years of treating him as a casual friend. He asks Krishna to bear with him, just as a father would with a son, a friend with a friend, or a lover with the beloved. The verse shows that we take those closest to us for granted, and it is never too late to ask for grace. It turns gently toward your night, where the mind replays a fight that is already over.",
      "for_you": "The fight with your daughter is over. You can let the words drop now, as a parent or friend would. You do not need to replay them to find a better ending."
    },
    "yv.1.16.22": {
      "meaning": "Rama is speaking to the sages. He tells them that his mind swoops onto tasks like a bird onto meat, and then a moment later loses interest, like a child with a toy. He is naming honestly what feels empty. The verse shows that naming what feels empty is the first step, and Rama's words are a question, not the answer. It turns gently toward your night, where the mind pounces on a fight and then drops it, leaving you restless.",
      "for_you": "Your mind is pouncing on the fight. See it for what it is: a restless mind looking for something to hold. You do not have to follow it into the dark."
    },
    "up_kath.1.3.5": {
      "meaning": "Yama is speaking to Nachiketa. He says that for one who lacks understanding, whose mind is never held steady, the senses run out of control like a driver's wild horses. For one who has understanding, whose mind is always steady, the senses obey like good horses. The verse shows that you must hold the reins of your mind, or your senses will drive for you. It turns gently toward your night, where the mind runs wild and the body lies still.",
      "for_you": "Your mind is running wild. You can hold the reins. Let your body rest now, and let the mind follow."
    }
  }
}

{
  "days": {
    "bg.2.58": {
      "meaning": "Arjuna is asking what a steady person looks like, and Krishna answers. He says it is like a tortoise pulling its limbs in. A person takes their senses away from the things that pull at them. When they do this, their wisdom becomes steady. The mind is restless because it dwells on things. The next time you find yourself replaying the fight, you can see that the trouble is in what you are dwelling on. The verse suggests gently pulling your attention back, just as the tortoise does.",
      "for_you": "When the fight replays after lights out, see it as your senses wandering toward the words. You can pull your attention back to the feeling of the bed beneath you. Let your limbs rest. Let your mind settle into the room, not the argument."
    },
    "bg.6.26": {
      "meaning": "Krishna is teaching Arjuna how to settle the mind. He says the restless mind wanders, and wherever it goes, you bring it back. You do this again and again. The mind is not your enemy; it simply wanders. The practice is to gently guide it back to where you want it to be. It is a matter of returning, not of forcing it to stay.",
      "for_you": "When your mind wanders back to the fight, gently bring it back to the room. You do not need to fight the thought. You simply notice it and return to the quiet. Do this again and again."
    },
    "ram_gita.46": {
      "meaning": "Lakshmana asks Rama how to cross the sea of confusion. Rama tells him to sit in a quiet place, let the senses rest, and give full attention to one thing. The mind becomes still like a calm ocean. Until understanding comes naturally, you rest in the Self. It is a practice of quiet sitting and steady faith.",
      "for_you": "Find a quiet place, even if it is just the bathroom after everyone sleeps. Let your senses rest. Give your full attention to one thing, like the sound of the fan. Let your mind rest there until it settles."
    }
  }
}

{
  "days": {
    "yv.p.janaka": {
      "for_you": "King Janaka did not leave his palace after his awakening. He simply returned to his duties with a mind that no longer clung. Your life will not wait for you to finish planning. You can sit at your desk tomorrow morning with the same open hands, letting the phone rest while you answer one email."
    }
  }
}

{
  "days": {
    "bh.vairagya.62": {
      "meaning": "Bhartrihari sits with his own mind, which will not stop. He asks why it wanders, why it replays the past or scripts the future. He tells it to rest somewhere. Whatever is to happen will happen just as it will, and not otherwise. He will take what comes and enjoy the pleasures that arrive unlooked-for. The verse proposes that the calm you want is already inside, waiting for you to stop rehearsing outcomes.",
      "for_you": "Your mind is planning the Diwali party long after the children are asleep, rehearsing every detail. The verse asks you to let the planning rest for now. Whatever happens will happen just as it will, and you will handle it. For tonight, close the planning and let the quiet come."
    },
    "viv.1.wait_and_watch": {
      "meaning": "Swami Vivekananda tells his students that the first lesson is to sit and let the mind run on. The mind is like a monkey jumping about. Let the monkey jump as much as it can; you simply wait and watch. But you will find that each day the mind's vagaries are becoming less and less violent, that each day it is becoming calmer.",
      "for_you": "After the children are asleep, your mind is the monkey jumping about. You do not need to fight it. Just sit and watch it jump. Let it run on for a few minutes without trying to control it. You will find it becomes calmer each day."
    },
    "ram_gita.46": {
      "meaning": "Rama sits with Lakshmana after the war. He tells him to sit somewhere quiet, let the senses rest, and give full attention to one thing. He says one should contemplate the One with undivided attention, resting in the Self. The verse proposes that quiet sitting and steady faith carry you until understanding becomes natural.",
      "for_you": "Your mind is restless, running through the party and the next day. The verse asks you to sit somewhere quiet, let your senses rest, and give your full attention to one thing. Just for a few minutes, let your mind rest in the quiet. This is enough for tonight."
    }
  }
}

{
  "days": {
    "up_maha.109": {
      "meaning": "Nidagha is back from his journey and he is tired. He tells his teacher Ribhu that his mind will not stop. It runs everywhere for no reason, like a stray village dog. The verse says that the first step is to see this clearly. Naming the restlessness is where peace begins.",
      "for_you": "You are lying in the dark, and your mind is already at the party venue. You do not have to stop the thoughts. You only have to see them for what they are, a mind that runs here and there for nothing."
    },
    "bg.6.19": {
      "meaning": "Krishna is speaking to Arjuna about the practice of meditation. He says that a mind which has been brought under control is like a lamp in a windless place. It does not flicker. The idea is that you do not need to force it still. You only need to return it again and again to one point.",
      "for_you": "When the thought of the party comes, do not push it away. Just bring your attention back to the feeling of your body in the bed. Return to that, again and again. That is your steady place."
    }
  }
}

{
  "seat": "the adult child whose parents share their private life with relatives",
  "scores": {
    "bg.3.35": 0.1,
    "bg.2.7": 0.3,
    "bg.12.15": 0.1,
    "bg.7.14": 0.3,
    "bg.11.44": 0.0
  }
}

{
  "days": {
    "yv.p.ram_despondency": {
      "for_you": "Rama lost interest in his duties and his own laughter when he returned home. He was not ill, but he was asking deeper questions. Your withdrawal from the daily pressure of your parents' calls is not a sign of weakness. It is the beginning of a necessary questioning. Let that quiet space help you see what is truly yours and what is not."
    }
  }
}

{
  "days": {
    "ashtavakra.18.55": {
      "meaning": "Ashtavakra is finishing his long talk with King Janaka on the nature of peace. He is describing the person who has found it. The wise person is mocked and scolded by servants, children, spouses, grandchildren and relatives, and they are not the least disturbed. Calm is not found by running away or changing places. It is the way you hold ordinary life. You can feel the sting of the words, and still your mind does not break.",
      "for_you": "When the phone rings and your parents share your private life with relatives, you feel the sting of the words. Let the sting be there. Your mind does not have to break. You can feel the disrespect and still hold your ground inside."
    },
    "vr.4.22.23": {
      "meaning": "Vali is dying. He has just lost his kingdom and his life to his brother Sugriva. He is giving his young son Angada advice on how to behave at Sugriva's court. Do not be too familiar, and do not be distant either. Both are serious mistakes. Keep a sense of the middle.",
      "for_you": "You are in a hard place with your parents. You can set a boundary without becoming cold. You can speak your truth without starting a war. Find the middle. It is a steady place to stand."
    },
    "mbh.13.161.111": {
      "meaning": "Bhishma is lying on his bed of arrows, answering Yudhishthira's questions on how to live a long and good life. He speaks of everyday conduct. Never speak ill of others. A person who wants to prosper should never stir up anyone's anger. A cruel word lodges in the heart.",
      "for_you": "You have asked your parents to stop sharing your private life. You can rest in that request. You do not need to stir up more anger today. Your words have already been spoken. Let them settle."
    }
  }
}

{
  "days": {
    "up.p.prashna.year": {
      "for_you": "You are waiting for one person to say you are ready. But readiness is not a verdict from your manager. It is a quality you build in yourself. Your promotion is not the only measure of your worth. Let your own discipline be the proof."
    }
  }
}

{
  "days": {
    "bg.12.19": {
      "meaning": "Krishna is describing the person he loves most. He says this person takes praise and blame as the same thing. They are quiet, content with whatever comes, and do not cling to status. They are steady in their mind and full of devotion. This kind of steady kindness is what is loved. It is a picture of a mind that does not need to prove itself to anyone else.",
      "for_you": "That review is one person's opinion, not your whole future. Let the words be what they are. You can still do your work and build your career without letting that one comment decide your worth."
    },
    "bg.6.26": {
      "meaning": "Krishna is teaching Arjuna how to meditate. He says that wherever the restless mind wanders, you must gently bring it back again and again. You do not fight it or force it. You just guide it back to where it needs to be. This is how you settle the mind and find steadiness.",
      "for_you": "When your mind goes back to the review, gently guide it back. Do not scold yourself. Just notice where it went and bring it back to what you are doing right now."
    },
    "bh.vairagya.62": {
      "meaning": "Bhartrihari is talking to his own mind. He asks why it wanders around in vain. He tells it to rest. He says he will take what comes, without replaying the past or scripting the future. He enjoys the moments that arrive without him looking for them. This is the peace that comes from letting go of the need to control the outcome.",
      "for_you": "Stop replaying the review. The future is not decided by one meeting. Let go of the need to prove yourself right now. Find rest in what is in front of you."
    }
  }
}

{
  "days": {
    "bg.6.7": {
      "meaning": "Krishna is speaking to Arjuna about being your own friend. He says that if you have conquered yourself and are at peace, your deeper self stays steady. It does not shake in heat or cold, in pleasure or pain, or in praise and insult. The idea is that you can be your own best friend or your own worst enemy. The choice is yours, and a steady mind is the mark of the one who has chosen well.",
      "for_you": "The review is not the fight. It is your mind choosing to be your own enemy by replaying it. Let your deeper self stay still today. Do not let one person's words decide your worth."
    },
    "yv.5.66.34": {
      "meaning": "Bhasa is speaking to Vilasa, his old friend, who admits he never found peace. Vilasa says his mind is caught on endless waves of tasks. It never rests because it is always thinking about what was done, what is being done, and what must be done next. The mind faints under this constant weight of reckoning.",
      "for_you": "Your mind is caught in a net of reckonings about the review and what comes next. Let the list rest for a moment. The review is one thing, not a lifetime of must-dos."
    },
    "mbh.5.33.33": {
      "meaning": "Vidura is speaking to the sleepless King Dhritarashtra. He says a wise person does not swell with praise or burn at a slight. They stay unruffled, like a deep pool of the Ganga. The idea is to stay deep and still, so you can decide calmly and act steadily.",
      "for_you": "The review is a slight, but it does not have to burn you. Stay deep and still like a river pool. Let the words pass over you without shaking your ground."
    }
  }
}

{
  "days": {
    "vr.p.vishvamitra_menaka": {
      "for_you": "Vishvamitra did not blame the nymph for his lost decade. He simply noticed the hours were gone and began again. You can look at the empty morning the same way. Notice what happened, let go of the shame, and start with one small page."
    }
  }
}

{
  "days": {
    "up_maha.180": {
      "meaning": "Ribhu is speaking to Nidagha, who is looking for freedom. Ribhu tells him that no drink or wealth can match the calm inside. He says that sorrow and worry only leave when the mind is still. Restlessness is just the mind's habit, and only your own mind can quiet it. The quiet you find inside is the real treasure.",
      "for_you": "Your phone is not the enemy. The restless wanting is. When you close the app, you are not fighting a device. You are choosing to let your mind settle, just for one minute, and see what happens next."
    },
    "vr.5.1.94": {
      "meaning": "Hanuman is flying across the ocean. A mountain rises to offer him rest. He thanks the mountain for its kindness, but he keeps flying. He says his work is waiting and he promised not to stop halfway. He stays focused on the task ahead, even when a comfortable offer comes his way.",
      "for_you": "When you open your phone tonight, you can say thank you to it for what it is, and then keep walking toward your books. The work is waiting, and your promise to yourself is what matters today."
    },
    "bg.3.34": {
      "meaning": "Krishna is telling Arjuna that every sense has two hidden enemies. They are attraction and aversion. They sit behind every sight and sound, waiting to take the wheel. He tells Arjuna not to let them drive. They are the real obstacles on the path.",
      "for_you": "The pull to open Instagram is an enemy waiting behind your eyes. You do not have to follow it. You can see the pull, and then choose to drive the car yourself, just for this one evening."
    }
  }
}

{
  "days": {
    "yv.1.16.3": {
      "meaning": "Rama sits with the sages and speaks of his own restless mind. He tells them the mind is like a wicker basket in water. You can pour in all the riches in the world, and the basket still never fills. It simply takes what is given and lets it wash through. He is naming the emptiness he feels, and he does not yet know how to fill it.",
      "for_you": "You open your phone for one minute and the night slips away. The wanting moves from one post to the next, never settling. This verse names that emptiness. See the basket for what it is, and do not blame the water for passing through."
    },
    "bg.2.55": {
      "meaning": "Krishna answers Arjuna's question about the steady person. He describes someone who gives up chasing every desire. When a person finds contentment within, by the self alone, their wisdom becomes steady. Krishna is tracing how attention leads to anger and ruin, and how guarding it builds steadiness.",
      "for_you": "Your attention is the gate. When you guard it and do not chase every new thing, you build steadiness. This means choosing one thing to focus on, even for a short while, and letting the rest pass by."
    }
  }
}

{
  "days": {
    "vr.p.sita_chooses": {
      "for_you": "Sita chose to go with Ram into the wilderness, not to leave her home, but to choose her own path. You are not required to walk into danger alone. Your choice to leave the room, to call a friend, or to seek safety is just as real, and it belongs to you."
    }
  }
}

{
  "days": {
    "tulsi_vp.250": {
      "meaning": "Tulsidas is frightened by the cruelty of the times. He tells Ram that he trusts and loves him, yet he is afraid. He asks Ram to speak the words he needs to hear. Ram tells him he is his. He says do not lose heart, do not tremble.",
      "for_you": "When the shouting starts, let the thought stay with you that you are not alone in your fear. Let it be your anchor. You do not have to stand still. Let it steady your hands enough to move toward the door."
    },
    "yv.5.21.9": {
      "meaning": "Vasishtha speaks to Rama after the story of two grieving brothers. He says take discernment as your one true friend and wisdom as your seasoned companion. Then you can move through life and not lose your way in crises. He means grief is love, but endless rumination is the part we can learn to stop feeding.",
      "for_you": "Today, when the memory of the shouting comes back, do not follow it down the dark path. Instead, turn your attention to the next small task. Let the weight of what was be held by the quiet of what is."
    },
    "tulsi_dv.96": {
      "meaning": "Tulsidas speaks to his own mind. He says tell the kind one everything, good and bad. The heaviness thins, and peace grows. He means when you lay your burden down, it becomes lighter.",
      "for_you": "Find one person you trust, even if it is just for a short call. Tell them the truth of your day. Let the words leave your mouth and see what space opens up in your chest."
    }
  }
}

{
  "days": {
    "bg.1.29": {
      "meaning": "Arjuna stands in his chariot and sees his own family lined up against him. He is about to fight a war he cannot bear to fight. His body gives way, his mouth goes dry, his limbs shake, and his hair stands on end. The text shows us a man frozen by grief and fear, not a hero stepping forward. It says that even the strongest among us can simply stop when the cost is too high.",
      "for_you": "You feel this in your body when the shouting starts and the door closes. Let the shaking be there. It is not a sign of weakness. It is your body telling you the cost is too high.",
      "mbh.12.27.12": {
        "meaning": "The sage Ashma speaks to King Janaka, who is struggling to find his footing after losing everything he built. Ashma says that mental pain comes from only two places. Something unwanted happened, or your mind is spinning and confused. There is no third source. Days do not come back. Grieve what is gone, then steady yourself with the people and the truth that are still in front of you.",
        "for_you": "Your mind is spinning between the fear of tonight and the pressure to adjust tomorrow. Name the two things you cannot control. Then look at the one person in front of you and steady yourself there, even if it is just for a moment.",
      "vr.3.66.5": {
        "meaning": "Ram is alone in an empty hut after Sita has been taken. He breaks down and rages, ready to destroy the whole world to find her. Lakshman steps in and says that trouble touches everyone. It flares up like fire and passes in a moment. A steady friend can stop grief from turning into destruction.",
        "for_you": "The fire of this tension will flare up and pass. You do not have to let it burn down everything around you. Let someone steady you, even if it is just the truth of your own breath, so you do not act out of pure grief."
      }
    }
  }
}

}

{
  "days": {
    "mbh.12.27.12": {
      "meaning": "King Janaka asks Ashma how to stay steady when people and wealth come and go. Ashma tells him mental pain comes from only two places. It is either something unwanted that happened, or a confused, wandering mind. There is no third source. He is saying that your pain is real. It comes from the violence you did not want, and from a mind that is spinning with fear. The days that have passed will not return. Grieve what you have lost. Then steady yourself with the people still in front of you. The truth underneath is that your mind needs a place to land, even in the middle of chaos.",
      "for_you": "Your mother-in-law says you should adjust, and your husband's violence makes your mind spin. Today, name one thing you cannot control and one person you can trust. Let your mind rest on that one steady thing for a moment."
    },
    "vr.3.66.5": {
      "meaning": "Sita has been taken. Ram is lost in grief and rages that he will destroy the world. Lakshman finds him and says take heart. Which living being does trouble not touch? Trouble strikes like fire and passes in a moment. He is telling Ram that grief is a fire that burns everything, but it does not last forever. A steady friend can stop that fire from consuming everything. The truth is that you are not alone in this pain, and it will not burn you forever.",
      "for_you": "Your husband's anger feels like a fire that could destroy everything you hold dear. Today, let someone steady you. Tell one person you trust how you feel. Let them be the one who reminds you that this pain will pass."
    }
  }
}
